			<div class="content" id="post-content-4822771">
									

<p>+1 because it's a fun story as it is, but seconding all of SirPudding's comments above.<sup class="footnoteref"><a id="footnoteref-911186-1" href="javascript:;" class="footnoteref" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnote-911186-1')">1</a></sup> This could be much, much stronger as both scifi and pulpy old-school horror with some tinkering.</p>
<p>For instance, on the technical side it's easier to get into orbit around Jupiter than around Mercury (short version is that Mercury doesn't help you brake), and the farther you get from the Sun - and to a lesser extent, planets - the better readings you're going to get from any sort of deep-space observatory. A station in solar orbit between Jupiter and Mars would be relatively easy to set up with the help of some lagrangian points, and put your station in empty space, any planets just distant points of light. (That's good for both observatories and for creep factor)</p>
<p>On the narrative side, I think a slower and more subtle buildup would amplify the creep factor exponentially. On this front, I could actually see some really interesting potential if you were to change it to be affecting the ground crew of a probe- say, they're operating the thing from an isolated antenna station in the middle of nowhere, and events gradually escalate from cafeteria discussions about weird signals and if anybody heard a dog barking last night, to security cam footage of a hallway door acting like someone opened an airlock.</p>
<div class="footnotes-footer">
<div class="title">Footnotes</div>
<div class="footnote-footer" id="footnote-911186-1"><a href="javascript:;" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnoteref-911186-1')">1</a>. With the exception that Dr. Weir in <em>Event Horizon</em> is artisan ham of the finest cut.</div>
</div>

